<h2>The incident and the trial</h2>
<p>On 25 February 2020, during the violence in North-East Delhi, a 22-year-old man named Monish was set upon by a mob near Brijpuri and Chand Bagh. He died at GTB Hospital; the post-mortem attributed death to shock caused by blunt-force injuries to the brain. The FIR — No. 87/2020 of PS Dayalpur — invoked Sections 147, 148, 149, 427, 436 and 302 IPC along with the Prevention of Damage to Public Property Act. After trial in SC No. 199/2021, the Additional Sessions Judge convicted five men — Aman Kashyap, Devinder Kumar, Pradeep Rai, Ashish @ Goli and Arun Kumar — under Sections 148 and 304 Part I read with 149 IPC, sentencing each to seven years\' rigorous imprisonment on the principal count.</p>

<h2>The evidentiary architecture under attack</h2>
<p>The appeals targeted the familiar pressure points of a riot prosecution. The eyewitness foundation rested mainly on three police witnesses — PW-10, PW-12 and PW-13 — who deposed that the appellants led the armed mob. A public witness, PW-1, partly retracted his earlier account; PW-5 identified no accused. No test identification parade was held, and the police witnesses\' statements were recorded on 1 April 2020, more than a month after the incident. The defence pressed each of these as fatal.</p>
<p>The High Court dealt with them in sequence. PW-1\'s partial retraction did not erase his continuing identification of the appellants as members of the mob, so his evidence retained limited value. The police witnesses could not be discarded for their uniform: their accounts corresponded, and duty records placed them at the scene. On identification, the Court held that the absence of a TIP does not render dock identification inadmissible — it calls for caution, and here PW-10\'s prior acquaintance with the appellants supplied the assurance a parade might otherwise have given. The delay in recording statements was only partly explained by the riot conditions, but it neither discredited the occurrence nor suggested false implication; discrepancies about the exact place of assault, the PCR call, CCTV footage and call records were not decisive.</p>

<h2>Section 149: the common object, not the fatal hand</h2>
<div class="note">
<p>The judgment restates the settled architecture of constructive liability. Membership of the unlawful assembly and its common object could be inferred from the weapons carried, the exhortations attributed to the appellants, and their conduct during the assault. Once that inference stood, liability under Section 149 did not depend on proving which member inflicted the fatal injury. And the manner of the assault — repeated head blows after the victim had fallen — justified the finding under Section 304 Part I of an intention to cause bodily injury likely to cause death.</p>
</div>

<h2>What the dismissal signals for riot-case appeals</h2>
<p>Appeals in the 2020 violence cases have produced outcomes in both directions, turning almost entirely on the quality of identification evidence. Where identification has rested on untested footage or witnesses without prior acquaintance, acquittals have followed; where consistent eyewitness accounts survive cross-examination and are anchored by duty records or prior familiarity, convictions stand. This judgment sits firmly in the second category. For appellate practitioners, the lesson is that attacking police testimony in the abstract achieves little — the appeal must demonstrate concrete contradictions between the witnesses, the contemporaneous record and the medical evidence, and this record offered too few.</p>
<p>The appeals were dismissed in their entirety, and the convictions and sentences stand as imposed by the trial court.</p>
